WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.560
(upbeat music)

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.470
- All right everybody,
let's wrap this hour up

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.030
with one last feel good story.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.930
I am excited to share with
you, this is "What I'm Liking."

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.740
I am always deeply
impressed when young people

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.750
see issues in their
community and instead of

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.450
just pointing it out, they
actually do something.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.887
That's completely the case
for For 20-year-old Akayla,

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:23.749
after years of leading projects
and activities for kids

00:00:23.749 --> 00:00:25.688
in her Philadelphia neighborhood,

00:00:25.688 --> 00:00:28.779
she just opened a full on
community resource center.

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:29.730
So cool.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.640
We have her joining us
now from "Dimplez 4 Dayz."

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.327
Everybody say, "Hi!" to Akayla.

00:00:34.327 --> 00:00:35.610
Hi!

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:36.976
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:00:36.976 --> 00:00:39.570
- Hey guys, hey Kelly.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.120
- Oh my gosh, you do
have dimples for days.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.062
They're so cute.

00:00:43.062 --> 00:00:46.140
Oh my God, you're so cute.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.710
Okay, so you've been doing
nonprofit work since you were 13?

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.560
- Yes. It all started
when I was 13 years old.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.750
It literally was like, I was dancing

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.730
on the app called Dub Smash,

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.770
and then I started getting
all of these followers

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.371
outta nowhere and my mom was like,

00:01:00.371 --> 00:01:03.201
"what are you gonna do with
all of these followers?"

00:01:03.201 --> 00:01:04.710
And I really didn't know,

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.222
I just knew I always
wanted to help people.

00:01:07.222 --> 00:01:09.300
And she was like, well, it
sounds like you wanna be

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.681
in the nonprofit world.

00:01:10.681 --> 00:01:14.400
At 13 I didn't really know
what the nonprofit world was,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.190
but my mom had prior knowledge in it

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:18.949
so she was able to guide me.

00:01:18.949 --> 00:01:22.560
We did know that we wanted our mission

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.110
to be helping the community,

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.400
being able to give back to the community,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.070
provide resources, and
doing things like that.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:29.903
So we took off.

00:01:29.903 --> 00:01:33.000
We were doing book bag
drives, feeding the homeless,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.291
giving breakfast to the kids,

00:01:34.291 --> 00:01:36.930
and from right there, that's what it was.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.600
And like you said, I do have my dimples,

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.700
so the name was very much natural.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.350
People were calling me Dimples already.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.350
So with Dimplez 4 Dayz,
that's what it was.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.450
And that's where how
we got our mission too,

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.700
keeping the smiles and
the hearts and minds

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:51.533
of the community.

00:01:51.533 --> 00:01:52.860
Because every time we came around

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.681
people were always smiling.

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:56.803
- I love that.

00:01:56.803 --> 00:02:00.330
So now you actually have a brick
and mortar resource center.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.270
So what new opportunities
has that opened up?

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.030
- You gotta understand,
like coming from Philly,

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.470
there's a lot of violence in Philly.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.550
So with the resource center, yeah,

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.230
we're not just this resource center

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.810
that you could come in
and work on your resume.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.910
No, you could come in and you can

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.470
get those resources that you need.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.120
You could come in, you
can get something to eat,

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.440
you can sleep.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.950
You know, it's a safe haven for our youth.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.110
We don't even call them
youth, we call them Dreamers,

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.550
because it's a place that

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.310
you're turning your dreams into reality.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.430
But we have like certain
days for certain things

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:36.691
for our Dreamers to do.

00:02:36.691 --> 00:02:38.988
So on Monday, they'll like paint

00:02:38.988 --> 00:02:41.400
to work on their mental health.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.950
On Tuesdays and Wednesdays,
we like to call those

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.281
workforce development days.

00:02:45.281 --> 00:02:48.540
Thursdays, that's Taco Thursdays,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.281
they're making their tacos.

00:02:50.281 --> 00:02:53.310
And then on Fridays,
those are our youth panels

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:54.451
or our teen socials.

00:02:54.451 --> 00:02:59.310
All of these are our youth-led activities

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:03.450
because we like to show our
youth that they are the leaders.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.070
They're leading the discussion.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.182
Not only is that building
their self-esteem,

00:03:07.182 --> 00:03:09.780
but that's keeping them
from out of the street.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:10.830
You get what I'm saying?

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:11.952
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:11.952 --> 00:03:13.830
- Keeping them somewhere
safe, in that safe haven.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.020
- And empowering them and
giving them confidence

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.531
and that they can do this.

00:03:17.531 --> 00:03:18.822
(audience applauding)

00:03:18.822 --> 00:03:20.370
And when someone believes in
you, that changes everything.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.020
Thank you so much, Akayla.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.070
Love your dimples.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:23.940
They make me smile.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.553
I'm liking your post right now, alright?

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.200
And that's not all, our
season long partner, Pilot,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.290
pin makers of G2, the go-to
pin of the Kelly Clarkson Show,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.650
want to honor truly unstoppable
people just like you, ma'am,

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.810
so they're gonna be writing
you a check for $1000.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.102
How's that sound?

00:03:41.102 --> 00:03:42.771
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:03:42.771 --> 00:03:45.923
- Thank you, thank you.

00:03:45.923 --> 00:03:48.022
- Thank you so much.

00:03:48.022 --> 00:03:49.770
And thank you to all our guests this hour,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.122
Emma Roberts, Ramon
Rodriguez, Dr. Buck Drummond,

00:03:53.122 --> 00:03:55.680
Reeny, Maggie, and Nickleback.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.683
Y'all, if you're not having a
great day, try and change it.

